{"post": {"ID": 456, "post_title": "Robots.txt for SEO: A Complete Guide", "post_excerpt": "Robots.txt tells search engines and AI crawlers which parts of your site they may crawl. What it does, what it cannot do, how to write one and how to test it.", "post_author": "3", "post_date": "2025-01-29 17:05:07", "post_modified": "2026-11-13 12:18:09", "post_content_b64": ""}, "meta": {"_thumbnail_id": "529", "_yoast_wpseo_title": "Robots.txt for SEO: A Complete Guide (2026) | Reinforce Lab", "_yoast_wpseo_metadesc": "Robots.txt tells search engines and AI crawlers which pages they may crawl. Learn what it does, what it cannot do, how to write one and how to test it.", "rl_updated": "2026-11-13", "_rl_carried": "a:3:{s:7:\"prod_id\";i:215748;s:4:\"from\";s:61:\"https://reinforcelab.com/robots-txt-for-seo-a-complete-guide/\";s:8:\"decision\";s:5:\"D-178\";}", "rl_type": "guide", "_yoast_wpseo_primary_category": "12", "rl_answer": "A robots.txt file is a plain text file at the root of your website that tells search engine and AI crawlers which pages and files they may crawl. It manages crawling, not indexing: to keep a page out of Google, use a noindex tag or a password instead.", "rl_takeaways": "Robots.txt controls which parts of your site crawlers may open. It lives at the root, such as example.com/robots.txt.\nIt does not keep a page out of Google. A blocked page can still be indexed if other sites link to it.\nGoogle supports four fields: User-agent, Disallow, Allow and Sitemap. It ignores crawl-delay.\nEach subdomain needs its own file, and Google reads only the first 500 kibibytes.\nAI crawlers such as OAI-SearchBot and GPTBot follow robots.txt too, so decide on purpose whether to allow them.\nTest the file in a private browser window and in the robots.txt report in Search Console after every change.", "rl_faqs": "Do I need a robots.txt file? | Not always. Without one, crawlers assume they may crawl the whole site. A small site with no private or duplicate sections can work without it. Most sites still add one to block admin pages and point to the sitemap.\nCan robots.txt remove a page from Google? | No. It only stops crawling. Google says a blocked page can still appear in results if other pages link to it. To remove a page, use a noindex tag, a password, or delete the page.\nWhere should I put my robots.txt file? | At the root of the site it is for, such as https://www.example.com/robots.txt. Crawlers do not look for it in folders. Each subdomain needs its own file.\nHow long do robots.txt changes take to work? | Google usually keeps a copy of the file for up to 24 hours. So a change can take about a day to be noticed. OpenAI also says its search systems can take about 24 hours to adjust.\nWhat happens if robots.txt returns an error? | If the file is missing (a 404 error), Google treats the site as having no crawl limits. If the server fails (a 5xx error), Google stops crawling the site for the first 12 hours, then uses the last good copy for up to 30 days.\nWhat is the maximum size of a robots.txt file? | Google reads the first 500 kibibytes of the file. Anything after that is ignored. If your file is close to that size, combine rules or move blocked content into one folder.\nShould I block AI crawlers in robots.txt? | It depends on your goal. Blocking OAI-SearchBot keeps your site out of ChatGPT search answers. Blocking GPTBot or Google-Extended only limits use of your content for AI training. If you want AI tools to cite you, allow the search crawlers.\nDoes Google support crawl-delay in robots.txt? | No. Google lists crawl-delay as a field it does not support. Google supports User-agent, Allow, Disallow and Sitemap.\nWhat is the best robots.txt for SEO? | There is no single best file, because the right rules depend on your site. A good robots.txt for SEO is short: it blocks admin areas, internal search and pages that make endless near-copies, leaves everything useful open, and lists your sitemap. Test it after every change.\nWhat does Disallow: / mean in robots.txt? | It tells the crawlers named in that group not to crawl any page on the site. It is useful on a private test site and a serious mistake on a live one. If your live site is missing from Google, check for this line first.\nDoes robots.txt affect crawl budget? | It can, on very large sites. Google says crawl budget matters mainly for sites with about 1 million pages or more, or about 10,000 pages or more that change daily. On those sites, blocking pages you never want crawled saves effort. Small sites usually do not need to worry about it.", "rl_sources": "Google Search Central: Introduction to robots.txt | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro\nGoogle: Create and submit a robots.txt file | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/create-robots-txt\nGoogle: How Google interprets the robots.txt specification | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/robots_txt\nGoogle: List of Google's common crawlers (Google-Extended) | https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawlers-fetchers/google-common-crawlers\nOpenAI: Overview of OpenAI crawlers | https://platform.openai.com/docs/bots\nIETF: RFC 9309, Robots Exclusion Protocol | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9309", "rl_g_glossary": "Crawler | A program a search engine or AI tool uses to read web pages. Also called a bot or spider.\nUser agent | The name a crawler gives itself, such as Googlebot. Rules in robots.txt are addressed to user agents.\nDisallow | A rule that tells a crawler not to open a path.\nSitemap | A file that lists the pages you want search engines to find.\nNoindex | A tag or header that tells search engines not to show a page in results.", "_yoast_wpseo_focuskw": "robots.txt", "rl_g_cta_service": "services/technical-seo-services", "rl_g_cta_after": "5", "rl_g_cta_head": "Not sure what your robots.txt is blocking?", "rl_g_cta_sub": "Reinforce Lab finds what stops search engines and AI crawlers from reading your pages, fixes it, and re-crawls to prove it.", "rl_g_cta_button": "See technical SEO services"}}